<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ListView xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <fullName>Gated_Call_For_Offers</fullName>
    <!--
        =====================================================================================
        THE OR IS LOAD BEARING. DO NOT COLLAPSE THIS BACK TO A SINGLE startsWith FILTER.
        =====================================================================================
        Widened 2026-08-10 (Tranche 4A, decision D26.1 finding 3). The widening is PURELY
        ADDITIVE: filter 1 is byte identical to the filter that shipped, so every historical
        row this view already returned is still returned. Filter 2 only adds rows.

        WHY FILTER 2 EXISTS.
        ExtractAddressQueueable.buildOutcomeSummary returns the single outcome label verbatim
        ONLY when the email produced exactly one outcome. Two or more outcomes go through
        buildMultiSummary, which returns:

            Multi-Property (2): 1 Not Routed (call for offers), 1 ...

        That string starts with 'Multi-Property', NOT with 'Not Routed', so filter 1 alone
        misses it. Today the U2 call for offers gate always adds exactly one outcome, so the
        case cannot arise. Tranche 4B emits one outcome PER MATCHED DEAL, so it arises on the
        first call for offers email that names two properties, and that email would silently
        vanish from this view.

        THAT MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS. This view is the ONLY surface for the call for
        offers misclassification watch. A confidently misclassified exclusive listing takes no
        registry claim, so a second broker who submits the same property later wins it
        outright, and recovering the first broker's protection is manual registry surgery.
        The window in which that is reversible is only visible here.

        WHY 'contains call for offers' IS THE CORRECT SECOND FILTER.
        buildMultiSummary composes each part as '<count> <label>', so the label text survives
        VERBATIM as a substring of the multi property summary. A contains test therefore
        catches the single property form, the multi property form, and any future sibling
        label that keeps the wording. It cannot over match: no other outcome label in
        ExtractAddressQueueable carries this phrase, and the class header forbids a call for
        offers label under the 'Not Acquisition' prefix (that prefix belongs to the separate
        deployed Gated_Not_Acquisition view).

        KNOWN RESIDUAL, NOT FIXABLE FROM A LIST VIEW.
        Outcome__c is Text(255) and the staging service clips to that length. A multi property
        summary long enough to be clipped can lose the trailing part naming the call for
        offers outcome, and this filter cannot see what was clipped away. Ordering helps but
        does not close it: buildMultiSummary orders by descending priority, so a matched deal
        outcome (PRIORITY_REPEAT) sorts ahead of an unmatched one (PRIORITY_NO_PROPERTY), and
        the phrase appears early. Raise this with the 4B owner if a 10 property blast is ever
        observed clipping.
        =====================================================================================
    -->
    <booleanFilter>1 OR 2</booleanFilter>
    <columns>NAME</columns>
    <columns>From_Address__c</columns>
    <columns>Subject__c</columns>
    <columns>Outcome__c</columns>
    <columns>Property_Count__c</columns>
    <columns>Processed_DateTime__c</columns>
    <filterScope>Everything</filterScope>
    <filters>
        <field>Outcome__c</field>
        <operation>startsWith</operation>
        <value>Not Routed</value>
    </filters>
    <filters>
        <field>Outcome__c</field>
        <operation>contains</operation>
        <value>call for offers</value>
    </filters>
    <label>Gated: Call for Offers</label>
</ListView>
